Subject: [PATCH 1/3] tests: tighten Q2_0 error limits and add Q2_0 dot/mul_mat
 cases

test-quantize-fns checks Q2_0 against the ternary limits (0.01 for
quantization error, 0.15 for dot product error), which are far above
what the 30-byte Q2_0 format reaches (0.0025 and 0.0148). Give Q2_0
its own limits, 0.005 and 0.03.

Those limits still use quantizer output, whose sub-block scales cover a
narrow range. Swapping two sub-block multipliers in the NEON kernel
gives a dot product error of 0.0137 (0.0148 unmodified), so it passes.
Add a test that runs the CPU Q2_0 x Q8_0 vec_dot on random block bytes
(random scales and indices; rows of 64, 1856 and 2688 weights) and
compares it with a double precision reference from the dequantized
values. The error normalised by sum|w*x| is about 5e-8 and the limit is
1e-5. The mutation above gives 0.03-0.3 and fails. The
test runs whichever kernel the build selects, so on a build without
dotprod (-DGGML_NATIVE=OFF -DGGML_CPU_ARM_ARCH=armv8-a) it covers the
armv8.0 branch.

test-backend-ops had only the default mul_mat shapes for Q2_0. Add
eval cases at the dimensions the 3B-active Nemotron model uses
(k = 64, 1856, 2688; m = 257, which is not a multiple of the rows a
Metal kernel handles per threadgroup; n = 1..128 so the mul_mv,
mul_mv_ext and mul_mm paths and the CPU 1x{8,4,3,2} tiling are all
hit) plus mul_mat_id with n = 1 and n = 64.

The quantizer produces sub-block scale nibbles in a narrow range (mostly
the high end), so those cases cannot show that the Metal kernel decodes
every multiplier correctly. Add mul_mat and mul_mat_id variants
(k = 64 and 1856, n = 1..32) that fill the Q2_0 weights with random raw
block bytes and a finite fp16 scale (14 more cases; 202 in total for
-p q2_0). Using max(nib, 4u) as the Metal sub-block nibble, i.e. treating scale
nibbles 0..3 as 4, passes all 188 quantizer-based cases and fails
8 of the new ones. In the perf list add
decode and small-batch shapes (n = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 8, 512) for Q2_0 and
Q4_0.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
